[comment encoding = UTF-8 /]

[**
Module contains queries with a String as receiver.
This includes queries for string manipulation and formatting
operations not available in the AQL standard library or
that provide a more convenient interface for common patterns
used across the generation templates.

Note: This module complements the AQL built-in string
operations (replace, replaceAll, indexOf, tokenize, etc.)
with additional utilities specific to the UCMIS generation
context.

Related modules:
- typeQuery: queries that retrieve type names as strings
- namedElementQuery: queries that produce string
  representations of named elements such as paths
  and qualified names
/]
[module stringQuery(
  'http://www.eclipse.org/uml2/5.0.0/UML',
  'http://www.eclipse.org/uml2/5.0.0/Types',
  'http://www.eclipse.org/uml2/5.0.0/UML/Profile/Standard'
)/]

[**
Returns the given text with consecutive empty or blank lines collapsed
into a single newline. Removes lines consisting only of horizontal
whitespace (spaces, tabs) between two line breaks.
Note that \\h (horizontal whitespace character class) is a Java regex extension —
it works in Acceleo since the underlying engine is Java's java.util.regex,
but it is not standard POSIX regex.

@param txt - The string from which empty lines are to be removed.
/]
[query public u_removeEmptyLines(txt : String) : String =
  txt.replaceAll('(\\r)?\\n\\h*(\\r)?\\n', '\n')
/]

[**
Returns a string consisting of n repetitions of char.

@param char The character or string to repeat
@param n The number of repetitions
/]
[query public u_repeatChar(char : String, n : Integer) : String =
  if n <= 0
  then ''
  else char + char.u_repeatChar(n - 1)
  endif
/]

[**
Ensures a string is valid and Turtle-friendly for use as the local
part of a CURIE or IRI: trims leading/trailing whitespace, appends a
hyphen if the trimmed result ends with a period (avoiding ambiguity
with Turtle's statement terminator), then strips all remaining
whitespace, including internal, matching the original template's
noWhitespace post-processor exactly.

Query equivalent of the iriEscape template in commonRdf.mtl, moved
here since it is a pure string transformation with no dependency on
template-specific machinery, and can then be called safely from
other queries as well as from templates.

@param txt - The text to escape.
@return The escaped text.
/]
[query public u_iriEscape(txt : String) : String =
  let trimmed : String = txt.trim() in
  let withSuffix : String =
    if trimmed.endsWith('.') then trimmed + '-' else trimmed endif
  in
  withSuffix.replaceAll('\\s+', '')
/]

[comment] Not use yet. Using this might be more robust. But the change is some work.[/comment]
[**
 * Returns a PlantUML-safe identifier for the supplied string.
 *
 * The identifier is normalized as follows:
 *
 * - null or empty input becomes 'unnamed';
 * - every character other than a letter, digit, or underscore is
 *   replaced with an underscore;
 * - an underscore is prefixed if the resulting identifier starts
 *   with a digit;
 * - an underscore is also prefixed if the result starts with a
 *   PlantUML-sensitive dollar sign or apostrophe after any future
 *   changes to the allowed-character rules;
 * - an identifier consisting only of underscores is retained as
 *   syntactically safe, unless the input was empty or null.
 *
 * The returned value is intended for use as a PlantUML alias or
 * internal reference. It is not intended to replace the human-readable
 * label of the UML element; use a quoted PlantUML display name for that.
 *
 * @param aString - The string to convert into a PlantUML identifier.
 * @return A normalized identifier suitable for use as a PlantUML alias.
 */]
[query public u_plantUmlIdentifier(
  aString : String) : String =
  if aString = null or aString = ''
  then
    'unnamed'
  else
    let normalized : String =
      aString.replaceAll('[^A-Za-z0-9_]', '_')
    in
    if normalized = ''
    then
      'unnamed'
    else
      if normalized.substring(1, 1).matches('[0-9]')
      then
        '_'.concat(normalized)
      else
        normalized
      endif
    endif
  endif
/]

[**
 * Converts arbitrary text into plain text suitable for use inside a
 * reStructuredText :abbr: role.
 *
 * The result deliberately removes reStructuredText inline-markup
 * delimiters instead of attempting to preserve nested formatting.
 * This prevents constructs such as ``text``, `*text*`, **text**, and
 * :role:`text` from being parsed inside the abbreviation role.
 *
 * Processing rules:
 *
 * - null or blank input becomes 'unnamed';
 * - backslashes are removed;
 * - backticks are removed;
 * - asterisks are removed;
 * - role markers are neutralized by removing colons;
 * - newlines and tabs become spaces;
 * - repeated whitespace is collapsed;
 * - leading and trailing whitespace is removed.
 *
 * @param aString
 *     Arbitrary text to place inside :abbr:`...`.
 *
 * @return
 *     Plain text that does not contain nested reStructuredText markup.
 */]
[query public u_rstAbbrText(
  aString : String) : String =
  if aString = null or aString.trim() = ''
  then
    'unnamed'
  else
    aString
      .replaceAll('[\\r\\n\\t]+', ' ')
      .replaceAll('\\\\', '')
      .replaceAll('`', '')
      .replaceAll('\\*', '')
      .replaceAll(' +', ' ')
      .replaceAll('=', ' ')
      .replaceAll('\\s+', ' ')
      .trim()
  endif
/]

[**
 * Converts arbitrary text into safe content for a reStructuredText
 * :abbr: role.
 *
 * The returned value is intended to be inserted between the role
 * delimiters, for example:
 *
 *     :abbr:`<result>`
 *
 * Processing rules:
 *
 * - null and empty input become 'unnamed';
 * - carriage returns, line feeds, and tabs become spaces;
 * - backslashes are escaped before other characters are escaped;
 * - backticks are escaped so they cannot start interpreted text;
 * - opening and closing parentheses are escaped so they remain literal
 *   characters instead of being interpreted as the abbreviation
 *   explanation delimiter;
 * - repeated whitespace is collapsed;
 * - leading and trailing whitespace is removed.
 *
 * @param aString
 *     Arbitrary text to use inside a reStructuredText :abbr: role.
 *
 * @return
 *     Text that can safely be placed inside :abbr:`...`.
 */]
[query public NOTOKu_rstAbbrText(
  aString : String) : String =
  if aString = null or aString.trim() = ''
  then
    'unnamed'
  else
    aString
      .replaceAll('\\\\', '\\\\\\\\')
      .replaceAll('[\\r\\n\\t]+', ' ')
      .replaceAll('`', '\\`')
      .replaceAll('\\(', '\\(')
      .replaceAll('\\)', '\\)')
      .replaceAll(' +', ' ')
      .trim()
  endif
/]

[**
Sanitizes a raw attribute name for use as an XML element name or
xml:id value, both of which must be a valid NCName. The '@' character
is not legal anywhere in an NCName, so this strips it out entirely --
needed for attributes named after JSON-LD reserved keywords (e.g.
"@value", "@language"), a modeling convention distinct from the
":"-based embedded namespace prefix convention (see
u_hasEmbeddedNamespacePrefix): here the leading character is a pure
JSON-LD marker carrying no information worth preserving, unlike an
embedded namespace prefix.

@param rawName - The raw attribute name to sanitize.
@return The name with any '@' characters removed.
/]
[**
Sanitizes a raw attribute name for use as an XML element name or
xml:id value, both of which must be a valid NCName. Two characters
are not legal anywhere in an NCName and get substituted:

- '@' is stripped entirely -- needed for attributes named after
  JSON-LD reserved keywords (e.g. "@value", "@language"), where the
  leading character is a pure JSON-LD marker carrying no information
  worth preserving (see u_hasEmbeddedNamespacePrefix's own doc
  comment for the contrast with the ':' case below).
- ':' is replaced with '_' -- needed for attributes using the
  embedded-namespace-prefix convention (e.g. "cdif:displayLabel",
  see u_hasEmbeddedNamespacePrefix). Unlike '@', the prefix here
  carries real information, so it is preserved (as "cdif_displayLabel")
  rather than stripped -- this is XSD's own pragmatic substitute for
  the CURIE the RDF-target formats produce for the same attribute,
  since XSD has no native namespace mechanism suited to per-attribute
  namespacing the way RDF's prefix:local syntax does.

@param rawName - The raw attribute name to sanitize.
@return The name with '@' removed and ':' replaced by '_'.
/]
[query public u_xsdElementName(rawName : String) : String =
  rawName.replace('@', '').replace(':', '_')
/]
